WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.480
You open a terminal, type
a command, and hit enter.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:12.290
It feels simple, but top-level programs
like Bash are entirely blind to physical

3
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.410
reality. They have no concept of what a
disk sector is or how to manipulate the

4
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.420
specific electrical signals
lighting up your monitor.

5
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.170
If every program tried to send competing
electrical signals directly to the hardware

6
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.280
at once, the result would be
immediate conflict and system failure.

7
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.680
To prevent this chaos, a strict intermediary
steps in, the operating system.

8
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.110
Without this silent manager, a computer
is just a volatile collection of

9
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:44.340
electronics. The operating system turns
it into a reliable functioning tool.

10
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.140
The operating system
has two main missions.

11
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.420
The first is managing resources.

12
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.800
It allocates CPU time, memory, and energy.

13
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.920
When multiple programs demand the exact same
resource at once, it acts as a referee,

14
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.000
actively resolving the conflicts.

15
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.040
The second mission is offering
a machine that is easier to use.

16
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.850
Instead of forcing programmers to
interact with hardware interrupts or raw

17
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.620
controller registers, it offers clean
abstractions, processes, files, and sockets.

18
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:18.610
By doing this, it replaces an impossibly
complex physical machine with an organized

19
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.890
programmable canvas. To see how a command
actually reaches the hardware, we have to

20
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.780
map the system from top to bottom.

21
00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.260
The journey begins at the top two layers.

22
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.490
Applications, where your programs and
bash live, sitting just above the system

23
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:38.010
libraries, like glibc. Together, these top
two layers operate in what is called user

24
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.460
mode. In user mode,
libraries have some freedom.

25
00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.850
They can handle simple tasks entirely on
their own, like gathering text into a local

26
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.950
buffer. But the moment a program needs to
physically write to the screen or ask for

27
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.110
more memory, it hits a hard limit.

28
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.720
This dividing line is
the privilege boundary.

29
00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.020
The only authorized way to cross this
boundary is by making a system call.

30
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.770
By trapping applications above this line
and stripping them of direct hardware

31
00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:10.020
access, the system establishes the strict
perimeter where all security checks begin.

32
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.850
Once the system call is made, the command
drops below the boundary and enters kernel

33
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.320
mode. Here, the kernel
takes absolute control.

34
00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.540
Crucially, the kernel is not
a process and it is not root.

35
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.420
It is the core software
managing the system.

36
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.190
It handles active protection duties,
enforcing file permissions and ensuring that

37
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.020
programs absolutely cannot
read each other's memory.

38
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.450
The kernel subsystems, like the scheduler
and memory manager, process the request

39
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.580
and hand it down to the
next layer, the drivers.

40
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:46.130
Finally, the command reaches the hardware
layer at the very bottom where physical

41
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:52.030
action is executed. This mandatory top-down
sequence guarantees both functionality

42
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.380
and strict security across the system.

43
00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.140
This exact layered architecture
extends far beyond a single laptop.

44
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.540
In data centers and
supercomputers, it dominates.

45
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.020
Since November 2017, every single
machine on the TOP500 list runs on Linux.

46
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.860
It is also in your pocket.

47
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.740
Android phones use the Linux kernel
while iOS runs on the XNU kernel.

48
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.630
Even cars and medical devices rely on
this setup, often using real-time micro

49
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.540
kernels like QNX to guarantee
exact timing and safety.

50
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:29.060
Across all these devices, the operating
system serves as the invisible translator,

51
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.300
seamlessly bridging the gap between
human intent and raw electrical power.

52
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.280
Let's review. Question one, which
two layers run in user mode?

53
00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:42.060
Applications and libraries.

54
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.540
Question two, what is the only way
to cross the privilege boundary?

55
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:54.680
A system call. Now open animation A1.1
in the course hub and run lab TD0.
